{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's primary claim conditions resilience on 'a positive lower bound on the marginal cost of architectural monoculture that exceeds the marginal gains from pooling internal redundancy,' yet this condition has no observable or operational metric for adjudication. The 'evidence that would distinguish' independent from merely-uncorrelated failures, as required by the problem, is not addressed by this marginal-cost inequality. Instead, SRC_001 offers counterconditions (e.g., formal verification of the control plane, common external dependency) that gesture at observable signals, but it never ties those signals back to the claimed cost-benefit threshold. Concretely: suppose the large service formally verifies its control plane (countercondition 2) — the claim says the analogy 'fails,' but the main thesis about marginal cost is left without a falsifiable criterion, since 'marginal cost of monoculture' is never operationalized. The problem demands evidence that distinguishes failure modes; SRC_001's governing condition is not itself evidence-distinguishable. Counterexample: a decentralized network where operators independently choose heterogeneous stacks (positive monoculture cost) yet still suffer correlated failure through a shared protocol-level bug (e.g., a common TLS library vulnerability across diverse stacks) — the claim's condition is met but resilience is not delivered, and the provided counterconditions do not anticipate protocol-layer shared causes.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Decentralized operators independently select different OS, hardware, and orchestration, satisfying positive monoculture cost. Yet all depend on the same TLS library (e.g., OpenSSL) for inter-operation. A latent Heartbleed-class bug causes correlated failure across the 'diverse' network. The large service, with strictly more internal redundancy and a formally verified control plane, is unaffected. SRC_001's condition (positive lower bound on monoculture cost) is met, but correlated failure still occurs, falsifying the claim that meeting this condition yields resilience."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues that because any two systems share some causal ancestry, 'truly independent' is operationally meaningless and the claim is 'unfalsifiable without specifying the scope.' But the problem explicitly asks the respondent to *identify what evidence would distinguish* independent from merely-uncorrelated failures. SRC_002's conclusion that the distinction is 'a choice of observational scope' collapses the very evidentiary question posed: if everything reduces to scope choice, then SRC_002 provides no candidate evidence (e.g., stress testing under isolated perturbations, dependency-graph analysis, controlled fault injection) that could *empirically* distinguish the two. Its only escape hatch — 'if a bounded definition is agreed upon prior' — offloads the problem's burden back onto the questioner rather than answering it. A counterexample: even under SRC_002's own 'isolated environments' countercondition (Earth and Mars), it concedes shared causes are severed only 'locally,' not absolutely (both still share physical laws, solar-system-scale events). By SRC_002's logic, even Earth/Mars isolation does not make 'truly independent' operationally meaningful, so its own countercondition fails to rescue falsifiability. This self-undermining means SRC_002 does not satisfy the problem's demand to identify distinguishing evidence; it denies the possibility in principle.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Two services are operated on Earth and Mars as SRC_002's countercondition suggests. A solar flare disrupts both. Under SRC_002's unbounded definition, this is a 'shared cause,' so the systems were never truly independent. Even the proposed countercondition cannot produce a case where 'truly independent' is operationally meaningful, demonstrating that SRC_002 fails to identify any evidence that could distinguish the failure modes."}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}